runsc: keep the sandbox below its cgroup's memory limit and kill in the Sentry

What: when the host memory cgroup that contains the sandbox is cgroup v2, has a
limit and cannot swap, runsc gives the Sentry that cgroup's memory.current,
memory.stat and memory.max. The Sentry's MemoryFile then refuses an allocation
that would take memory.current, less the cgroup's file cache (active_file +
inactive_file, which the host can reclaim), within --memory-limit-headroom
(default 16 MiB) of memory.max. A refusal starts a round of the Sentry's OOM
killer: it waits for the MemoryFile's releaser to decommit freed pages, and if
the allocation still does not fit, it kills the application process with the
highest badness, computed as Linux's oom_badness() does (resident set in pages
plus oom_score_adj thousandths of the limit in pages), and waits until its tasks
have released their memory and the releaser has decommitted it. Like Linux,
which spares the global init, it never chooses the root PID namespace's init,
whose end ends the sandbox, nor a process whose oom_score_adj is -1000; with
nothing left to kill, it lets the next 16 MiB be allocated, so that allocations
go on to the host's limit. The task whose page fault or system call was refused
waits for the round and retries, so it dies if it was chosen; a copy to
application memory that the Sentry makes for a system call or to deliver a
signal reports the refusal rather than EFAULT, so that it is retried the same
way. Each kill is counted, as Linux counts its own, in oom_kill of cgroup v1's
memory.oom_control, which this patch adds to every memory cgroup, for the
victim's cgroup and its ancestors. The cgroup on the host is read only when an
allocation would use more than half of the room left at the last reading, or
every allocation once within the headroom.

Nothing changes when the cgroup has no limit, when it may swap (memory.swap.max
is not 0 and the host has swap), when it is not cgroup v2, or with
--memory-limit-headroom=0, which a dev.gvisor.flag.memory-limit-headroom
annotation may also set.

Why: the application's memory is in the Sentry's memory file, a memfd that the
host charges to the cgroup as shmem. The stubs map it; they do not own it. When
the cgroup is full and cannot swap, the host's OOM killer takes the stub that
maps the most, and that frees nothing: the memory stays charged until the Sentry
has noticed the stub's death (3.5-5.5 ms after the kill), torn its process down
and punched the pages out of the memory file (the first decommit ends about 20 ms
after the kill; 540 MB took until 55-75 ms). Any charge in that window invokes
the OOM killer again. In 5 of 6 traced runs the Sentry's own Go heap faulted in a
new page 16-17 ms after the kill; a new process touching its memory faults again
every few milliseconds. Each further kill takes another stub (oom_score_adj 500
from patch 0002, against the Sentry's -998) and frees nothing either, until the
kernel kills a stub whose loss ends the container, such as its init's, or the
Sentry itself. With swap the host reclaims the memory file to swap instead, until
swap fills too: with Docker's default swap allowance, 7 of 20 sandboxes were lost
that way.

The copies matter as much as the faults: reported as EFAULT, a signal frame the
limit refused became a forced SIGSEGV, which ended the sandbox when it hit its
init (supervisord, taking SIGCHLD from the children the Sentry killed, in 2 of
13 runs of 400 processes beside a desktop), and a system call whose copy was
refused failed with EFAULT; Linux retries a fault in copy_to_user after its OOM
killer has run.

Keeping a headroom below the limit and choosing the victim in the Sentry is what
a guest kernel does in a VM: the application's memory never reaches the host's
limit, and the Sentry frees the victim's memory itself before anything else
needs it. Freeing a stub's memory faster after a host kill cannot close the
window, since the Sentry allocates while it handles the death. Returning the
Sentry's free heap to the host before each kill (runtime/debug.FreeOSMemory)
left what a process got unchanged, in alternating runs at 512 MiB and 4 GiB. 16
MiB of headroom covers what the Sentry charges between two readings: with 4 MiB,
300 processes of 16 MiB took the cgroup to its limit in 3 of 20 runs (peak
memory.current 568-572 MiB of 572), with 16 MiB in none (peak 556-562). A kill
the Sentry makes is no OOM kill of the host cgroup: its memory.events counts no
oom or oom_kill and Docker does not report the container as OOMKilled, so
memory.oom_control is where a reader in the sandbox learns of it, and sparing
init keeps the sandbox, and that record, alive after every kill the Sentry
makes.

gVisor issue 2533 (open since 2020) describes this race; upstream says an
in-sandbox OOM killer is being worked on (2026-11-17), and PR 17505 adds
allocation-time memory accounting for it. This patch goes once upstream ships
one.

Verified: with Orbit's flags (--memory M + 60 + M/1024 MiB with M = 512, i.e.
572m, --memory-swap equal to it, --oom-score-adj 500) in python:3.12-slim, the
workload started by docker exec, against runc with --memory 512m and
--memory-swap 512m; "before" is Orbit's runsc without this patch. "Lost" means the
container stopped; "host kills" are the OOM kills counted in the container's
cgroup on the host; "held" is what the allocator, touching 8 MiB at a time, held
when it was killed; "2nd" is a small process started beside the workload.

