Away from the limit, touching 1 GiB and 100000 4 KiB writes to /dev/shm took as
long with this patch as without it (g2, alternating, 5 runs each: 0.53-0.58 s
and 0.69-0.80 s with it, 0.54-0.58 s and 0.68-0.81 s without).

Not covered: memory the Sentry allocates on its own heap for the application
(socket and pipe buffers), the stubs' page tables, and pages the host commits
later into ranges allocated earlier but left untouched (anonymous memory is
allocated 2 MiB at a time and committed on first touch) are not seen at
allocation; if they reach the limit, the host's OOM killer acts as before. A
single allocation larger than the room left, such as fallocate(2) or
MAP_POPULATE of a large range, is refused whole: the round kills one process per
retry until it fits. A system call refused at the limit is run again from the
start after the round: what it had done before the refused copy stays done, and
its result for that is lost, as when EFAULT ended it. Killing processes frees
none of a tmpfs's memory: a tmpfs written full leaves the Sentry killing every
process but init and those at -1000, as Linux's OOM killer would, and then the
host's OOM killer. A process keeps about the headroom less memory than without
this patch (16-32 MiB by the medians, with the default headroom and the
allocator's 8 MiB steps). gVisor's cgroup v1 memory.oom_control is read-only:
the OOM killer cannot be disabled.

